<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helena of Adiabene</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 50 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helena was queen of Adiabene in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the 1st century CE, wife of King Monobazus I and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izates-ii">Izates II</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> she and her son converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. She settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, sent food to the city during a famine in the 40s CE, and was buried in the monumental tomb now called the Tomb of the Kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/helena-of-adiabene.jpg" alt="Helena of Adiabene" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hanay · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helena_of_Adiabene_Sarcophagus_1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Helena was queen mother of Adiabene, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>. Her husband Monobaz I was probably also her brother, and she was the mother of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izates-ii">Izates II</a> and Monobaz II. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> she was the daughter of King Izates, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> of Chorene makes her the chief wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abgar-v">Abgar V</a>, king of Edessa. She became a convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> around the year 30 CE.</p><p>What is known of her rests on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>, Movses Khorenatsi, Kirakos Gandzaketsi and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>. Josephus, though younger, was almost her contemporary: he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> while she lived and was buried there, and wrote much of his work from first-hand knowledge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>During a famine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 45–46 CE she sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> for grain and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> for dried figs for the sufferers, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>, in Bava Batra 11a, credits this to Monobaz II instead. The Talmud also records her gifts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a>: a golden candlestick over its door, whose reflected rays at sunrise told everyone that it was time to read the Shema, and a golden plate inscribed with the passage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> read when a wife suspected of infidelity was brought before the kohanim.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> tells that she vowed to become a Nazirite for seven years if her son Izates came back safe from war. When she went to Judea at the end of that time, the School of Hillel told her to observe the vow anew, and after it she became ritually impure and had to repeat it, so that she was a Nazirite for twenty-one years; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> bar Ilai, however, said fourteen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Helena was buried in the pyramidal tomb she had built in her lifetime, three stadia north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, whose catacombs are known as the Tombs of the Kings. A sarcophagus found there bears an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscription that scholars read as &quot;Our mistress, the Queen&quot;. Louis Félicien de Saulcy discovered it in the nineteenth century but took the remains for a queen of the First Temple period; the anger of the Jewish community of Jerusalem halted his dig, and the finds went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. Ruins believed to be her palace were excavated by Doron Ben-Ami in the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helena of Adiabene” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Josephus tells how Queen <b>Helena</b> and her son King Izates II adopted Judaism in the 1st century CE; Helena sent food to Jerusalem during a famine and was buried in the so-called Tomb of the Kings there, and Adiabenian princes fought for the Jews against Rome in 66…</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-adiabene">Kingdom of Adiabene</a></li></ul></section>
